  "body": "### Description\r\n\r\nSynapse is run via Docker. There is an existing homeserver.yaml file which is mounted into the container. The path is specified via `SYNAPSE_CONFIG_PATH`. \r\n\r\nEvery time i change the homeserver.yaml and re-upload it via ansible and restart the container via ansible, i get an error: `Environment variable SYNAPSE_SERVER_NAME is mandatory`\r\nIt doesnt matter if i set `SYNAPSE_SERVER_NAME` explicitly via docker env, it has still the same error. Of course, the homeserver.yaml has a `server_name` setting.\r\n\r\nOnly solution is to delete the container and recreate it. \r\n\r\n### Steps to reproduce\r\n\r\n- Install synapse via Docker, a homeserver.yaml file mounted in the Container, and `SYNAPSE_CONFIG_PATH` set. \r\n- When everything is running, upload a new homeserver.yaml file to the docker host\r\n- Restart the container so that it picks up the new homeserver.yaml\r\n\r\n### Version information\r\n\r\n- **Homeserver**: matrix.simonszu.de\r\n\r\nIf not matrix.org:\r\n\r\n\r\n- **Version**: \r\nDocker-Tag \"latest\".\r\n Image: https://hub.docker.com/r/matrixdotorg/synapse\r\n Image hash 8258b0d90727. \r\nHappens on other synapse images as well, e.g. the one from avhost. \r\n\r\n- **Install method**: Docker container, via ansible\r\n\r\n- **Platform**: \r\nDebian Linux in an LXC container on a Debian Hypervisor. The VM has sufficient RAM and storage, and is connected via IPv4 and IPv6. 64bit. \r\n",
